# Edge-Case Miner: build brief for Claude Code

You are helping Henri build a hackathon project today at the VAST Builders Challenge
(Real-Time Video Agents Hack, San Francisco). Submission deadline is 4:30 PM Pacific.
Everything must be working and demoable by then. Read this whole file before doing anything.

## What we are building

**Edge-Case Miner**: an agent that turns hours of indexed video into a verified, labelled
training dataset for physical AI from one plain-English request.

A user types something like "pedestrian crossing in front of a turning car" or "forklift passing
close to a person". The agent:

1. Searches every camera group in the archive for candidate clips.
2. Verifies each candidate by asking Cosmos Reason a yes/no question about the actual clip, and
   extracts structured labels (actor, action, distance class, lighting, weather, occlusion).
3. Scores clip quality (YOLO detections present, blur, event fully visible) and rejects bad clips.
4. Writes a coverage report (what we found, grouped by camera group, lighting, action) and a gap
   report ("nothing at night, nothing from the highway view") with a next-collection plan.
5. Exports the confirmed clips as a dataset: manifest, labels, clips, dataset card.
6. **The agentic part:** if too few clips pass, the agent writes a better ingestion prompt,
   re-ingests the most promising videos through the pipeline with that prompt, then searches and
   verifies again. The organisers' own guide says "the prompt decides what gets indexed"; this loop
   puts that idea in the demo.

Judging criteria are creativity, technical implementation and real-world impact. The judges include
NVIDIA's Cosmos and video-analytics people and CoreWeave's physical AI lead. The pitch: robotics and
autonomous-driving teams have hours of footage and no time to label it; one sentence in, a verified
dataset out, plus the list of what they still need to collect.

## The environment (read these first, do not guess)

The stack is prebuilt and already running. We build on top of it. Before writing any code:

1. `cd ~/vast-builders-challenge && git pull` (the pre-cloned repo may be behind).
2. Read `BUILD_DAY.md`, `ARCHITECTURE_REFERENCE.md`, `.cursor/rules/build-day.mdc`, and every
   `SKILL.md` under `.cursor/skills/retrieval/`, `.cursor/skills/ingest/reingest-videos/`, and
   `.cursor/skills/gpu/` (including `gpu/README.md`). These are the API reference. They are written
   for Cursor but the endpoints, request bodies and rules apply to you exactly the same way.
3. Load the team config: `set -a && source /config/*.config && set +a`. There is exactly one
   `/config/<team>.config` file. If it is missing, stop and ask Henri. Never print, log or commit
   its values. To list variable names only: `env | cut -d= -f1 | sort`. Never run bare `env`.

Key facts from the docs:

- Backend API base: `$INGRESS_URL/api/v1`. Login with `POST /auth/login` using `$USERNAME` and
  `$PASSWORD` to get a JWT. Every call uses `Authorization: Bearer <token>` except
  `/videos/stream` and `/videos/playback-url`, which take `?token=`.
- Search: `POST /search` with `query`, `top_k` (1 to 100), `min_similarity` (use 0.3 to 0.5),
  `metadata_filters` (`camera_id`, `location`, `capture_type`), `tags`, `time_filter`. Response has
  `results[]` (segment hits with `source`, `similarity_score`, `reasoning_content`, timing) and
  `chunk_results[]` (grouped by parent `original_video` with `best_match_start_sec/end_sec`).
- Q&A with evidence: `POST /agent/ask`, `POST /agent/search-and-answer`. Tools:
  `GET /tools/segments?original_video=`, `GET /tools/segment?source=`,
  `GET /tools/detections?source=`.
- Per segment: `GET /videos/metadata?source=`, `GET /videos/detections?source=` (404 means no
  YOLO sidecar, which is a signal, not an error), `GET /videos/stream?source=&token=` to download
  the clip. Browse: `GET /videos/explore?scope=all&limit=100&offset=0`.
- Filter values: `GET /metadata/schema`, `GET /metadata/values?field=camera_id`. Prompt presets:
  `GET /metadata/ingest-config`.
- Re-ingest: `POST /dashboard/reingest` with `original_video` (or `stream_id`), `chunk_count`,
  and `custom_prompt` (max 800 characters; overrides `scenario`). Returns `job_id`; poll
  `GET /dashboard/reingest/<job_id>` every 4 seconds until `status` is `completed`. Re-ingest
  REPLACES the segment descriptions; it does not add to them. The row does not store which prompt
  was used, so we keep our own log.
- Routes that do not exist: `/reports`, `/alerts`, `/analytics`, `/videos/ask`, `/tags`,
  `/locations`. Do not invent routes.
- GPU models are directly callable with `Authorization: Bearer $GPU_BEARER_TOKEN`:
  - Cosmos3-Reason at `$COSMOS3_REASON_URL/v1/chat/completions`, OpenAI-compatible. Video goes in
    as a content part: `{"type":"video_url","video_url":{"url":"data:video/mp4;base64,..."}}`.
    Get the model id from `GET /v1/models`. Empty `content` means the endpoint is overloaded.
  - YOLO11 at `$YOLO_URL/v1/infer` with `{"video_base64": ..., "filename": ..., "include_frames": true}`.
    Health is `GET /healthz` only.
  - Cosmos Embed1 at `$COSMOS_EMBED1_URL/v1/embeddings` (256-dim; `request_type: "query"`).
  - Canary-1B ASR at `$CANARY_1B_URL` (optional, not needed for this project).
- Our own reasoning LLM runs on Weights & Biases serverless inference using `$WANDB_API_KEY`,
  `$WANDB_TEAM`, `$WANDB_PROJECT`. It is OpenAI-compatible. Confirm the base URL, model names and
  required headers from https://docs.wandb.ai/inference before the first call; do not assume.
- Video corpus (camera_id in brackets): I-24 Nashville highway multi-cam [`i24_cam-1`], Toronto
  dashcam drives [`pie_cam-3`, six long sets], neighbourhood street cam [`neighborhood_cam-1`, two
  full days], SF street cams [`sf_streets_cam-1..4`, may still be ingesting], synthetic warehouse
  [`sdg_warehouse_cam-2`, about 178 short clips], indoor smart spaces [`smartspace_cam-1`, about
  102 clips]. Verify the live list with `/metadata/values?field=camera_id` on startup; it may have
  changed.

## Hard rules

- Never commit or print secrets. Add `cache/`, `clips/`, `exports/`, `*.log`, `.env` to `.gitignore`.
- Do not ingest video from the internet. Do not upload new footage. Only re-ingest what is indexed.
- Do not modify, redeploy or rebuild the DataEngine pipeline or any shared infrastructure.
- Use the skills' request formats before writing your own REST calls.
- Ask Henri before: starting any re-ingest (it is shared team infrastructure and takes minutes),
  deploying to Kubernetes, pushing to GitHub.
- Re-ingest with `chunk_count` 1 or 2 only, and only on chunks whose current captions came closest.
- Every re-ingest prompt must be a superset: a general scene description first, then the specific
  fields we need. Never a narrow prompt that destroys general searchability.
- Cosmos verification calls are bounded: at most 20 per query, concurrency 2, 60 second timeout,
  one retry. Cache every answer by (segment source, question) on disk. If Cosmos returns empty
  content, fall back to the stored `reasoning_content` and mark the verdict as "unverified".
- Keep the project runnable at the end of every phase. Commit after each phase.
- Web app or CLI only. No native apps (the VM is a browser session).

## Project layout

Create `edgecase/` in the repo root as a Python package. Keep dependencies small:
`httpx` or `requests`, `fastapi`, `uvicorn`, `opencv-python-headless`, `numpy`, `pydantic`.
`weave` is optional for logging; install it only if it installs cleanly in under a minute.

```
edgecase/
  config.py       # loads env, resolves endpoints, nothing printed
  vss_client.py   # login, search, segments, metadata, detections, stream download, reingest, poll
  llm.py          # W&B inference client: plan query, write labels schema, write prompts, write reports
  verify.py       # Cosmos3-Reason yes/no verification + label extraction on a clip, with cache
  quality.py      # YOLO sidecar check, blur score (Laplacian variance on 3 frames), completeness
  miner.py        # orchestration: request -> search -> verify -> score -> results
  loop.py         # the agentic re-ingest loop (propose prompt, pick chunks, reingest, poll, retry)
  report.py       # coverage matrix, gap report, next-collection plan
  export.py       # manifest.json, labels.csv, clips/, README dataset card, zip
  app.py          # FastAPI serving one HTML page + JSON endpoints
  __main__.py     # CLI: python -m edgecase mine "..." --groups warehouse --top-k 40 --verify 20
cache/            # verification answers, downloaded clips (gitignored)
exports/          # datasets (gitignored)
reingest_log.jsonl  # chunk, prompt, job_id, timestamps, before/after counts (committed; no secrets)
```

## Phases and time budget

Work phase by phase. Do not start a later phase until the earlier one runs end to end.
Tell Henri when each phase is done, in one or two sentences.

### Phase 0: Setup and health (target: 20 minutes)

- Pull the repo, read the docs listed above, source the config.
- Log in, call `/dashboard/stats`, `/metadata/schema`, `/metadata/values?field=camera_id`,
  `/metadata/ingest-config`. Print a short summary of what is indexed (counts per camera_id).
- Health-check Cosmos (`/v1/models`), YOLO (`/healthz`), Embed1 (`/v1/models`).
- Make one test call to W&B inference and one tiny text-only call to Cosmos ("Reply with OK").
- Run three searches on the warehouse clips ("forklift near a person", "person in a walkway",
  "pallet blocking an aisle"), print the top 5 `reasoning_content` for each so we learn what the
  default ingestion prompt captured. Report what is and is not in the captions (lighting? distance?
  actions?). This decides the re-ingest prompt later.
- Scaffold the package, `.gitignore`, `requirements.txt`. Commit.

### Phase 1: One clip end to end (target: done by 1:00 PM)

- `miner.py`: take a request, have the LLM turn it into a search query plus optional camera filters,
  run `/search` with `top_k` 40 and `min_similarity` 0.3 across all groups (or the groups asked for).
- `verify.py`: for the top N candidates (default 20), download the segment via `/videos/stream`,
  base64 it, call Cosmos3-Reason with a prompt of this shape:

  ```
  You are verifying a video clip for a training dataset.
  Question: Does this clip show <event>?
  Answer in this format:
  <think>your reasoning</think>
  <answer>YES or NO</answer>
  <labels>{"actors": [...], "action": "...", "distance_class": "contact|under_2m|2_to_5m|over_5m|none",
  "lighting": "day|dusk|night|indoor", "weather": "clear|rain|snow|fog|unknown",
  "occlusion": "none|partial|heavy", "event_fully_visible": true|false}</labels>
  ```

  Parse all three blocks. Temperature 0, max_tokens around 400. Cache the result.
- `quality.py`: `/videos/detections` for the sidecar (count of relevant classes; 404 lowers the
  score), blur score from 3 sampled frames with OpenCV, `event_fully_visible` from the labels.
  Produce a 0 to 1 quality score and a keep/reject decision with a reason.
- CLI prints a table: source, similarity, verdict, quality, reason, one-line Cosmos reasoning.
- Test on the warehouse group first (short clips, high signal), then on `pie_cam-3` with a
  pedestrian request. Commit.

### Phase 2: Coverage, gaps, export (target: 2:15 PM)

- `report.py`: coverage matrix of confirmed clips by camera group x lighting x action (and
  distance class). Have the LLM write a short gap report naming the empty cells that matter for the
  request, and a next-collection plan (three to five concrete lines).
- `export.py`: `exports/<slug>/manifest.json` (per clip: source URI, original_video, start and end
  seconds, labels, verdict and reasoning, quality scores, detections summary, similarity),
  `labels.csv`, `clips/*.mp4`, `README.md` dataset card (request, counts, coverage table, gap
  report, how it was produced, which models). Zip it.
- Commit.

### Phase 3: The agentic re-ingest loop (target: 3:00 PM)

- `loop.py`: if confirmed clips < target (default 10) after Phase 1:
  1. The LLM reads the request, the near-miss candidates' captions, and the current coverage, and
     proposes a superset ingestion prompt under 800 characters (general description first, then the
     specific fields the request needs: actions, distances, lighting, actors).
  2. Pick the one or two chunks (`original_video`) whose candidates scored highest but failed or
     were unverified. Show Henri the prompt and the chunks and wait for a yes.
  3. `POST /dashboard/reingest` with `chunk_count` 1, poll until completed, append to
     `reingest_log.jsonl` with before counts.
  4. Search and verify again, append after counts. At most 2 iterations.
- Measure how long one iteration takes. If it is reliably under 3 minutes, it can be run live in
  the demo; otherwise we record it. Tell Henri the number.
- Commit.

### Phase 4: Web UI (target: 3:30 PM)

- `app.py`: one HTML page served by FastAPI. Query box and group checkboxes. A run shows: progress
  (searched / verified / confirmed / rejected), a grid of confirmed clips with inline playback via
  `/videos/stream?source=&token=` proxied through our backend so the token is never in the page
  source, each with its labels and Cosmos reasoning; a separate "rejected" strip showing why (the
  rejection proves verification is real); the coverage heatmap; the gap report; an Export button;
  and the loop log (prompt proposed, chunks re-ingested, before and after counts).
- Keep the design plain and legible on a projector: large type, high contrast, no clutter.
- Optional only if everything above works: deploy with the organisers' `deployment/deploy-app-no-registry`
  skill at `/app`. Ask Henri first. A local `uvicorn` run is fine for the demo.

### Phase 5: Demo and submission (start no later than 3:30 PM)

- Write `README.md` at repo root: what it does, who it is for, architecture (search -> verify ->
  score -> report -> export, plus the re-ingest loop), which models and skills were used, how to
  run it, screenshots.
- Prepare a demo script for a two-minute walkthrough: one request, confirmed and rejected clips,
  coverage and gaps, export, then the loop (live or recorded).
- Follow `.cursor/skills/submission/SKILL.md` to draft `SUBMISSION.md` (description under 40 words,
  stack list, code link, live link if any). No personal details in that file.
- Ask Henri before pushing to the public GitHub repo. Make sure no secrets are in the history.

